  "narrative": "These new proposals address three remaining gaps: a plausible p53/USP7 axis, proliferation-dependent MYC effects on transcription/R-loop/mitotic dependencies, RTK-amplification/secretory-trafficking effects in adenocarcinoma, and whether accepted marker-dependency axes are modified by TP53 status. The global TP53-loss to USP7 test is motivated by the MDM2-p53-USP7 deubiquitination axis: TP53-loss lines may need less USP7 to protect p53, giving weaker USP7 dependency, i.e. a less negative score and a raw positive exposed-minus-comparator contrast encoded by direction +1. The MYC-by-high-proliferation tests ask whether MYC-amplified lines that are already dividing rapidly show stronger dependency on POLR2A, KIF18A, and DHX9, as predicted by transcriptional stress, mitotic stress, and R-loop resolution demands; direction -1 encodes stronger dependency in MYC-amplified lines. The TMED10 tests probe EGFR or ERBB2 amplification in adenocarcinoma because receptor tyrosine kinase signaling may increase secretory trafficking demand through TMED10; direction -1 again encodes stronger dependency. The RIT1 test examines EGFR amplification in KRAS-intact lines, where EGFR signaling through small RAS-family proteins might increase RIT1 dependency. Finally, the two interaction tests and the USP7/CDKN2A specificity control assess marker independence and context modification: whether TP53 loss modifies SMARCA4-loss or KEAP1-loss effects, and whether CDKN2A loss has a clean p53-wild-type, high-quality-screen USP7 effect. Clinical-significance thresholds remain approximately 10% of the absolute outcome mean; for example USP7 about 0.018, POLR2A/KIF18A/DHX9/TMED10 about 0.025 to 0.026, and SMARCA2 about 0.042. All estimates are already signed to the registered direction and are dataset-specific associations, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 10 exploration. Current accepted dataset-specific axes remain: MSI-high/WRN stronger dependency; SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 stronger dependency; KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 weaker dependency; PTEN-loss/STAT3 stronger dependency; CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 stronger dependency. Registered new follow-ups: USP7 with TP53 loss as a biologically plausible new axis; MYC-amplification effects in high proliferation on POLR2A, KIF18A, and DHX9; EGFR/ERBB2 amplification on TMED10 in adenocarcinoma; EGFR amplification on RIT1 in KRAS-intact lines; TP53-loss interactions with SMARCA4 loss and KEAP1 loss; and CDKN2A/USP7 in TP53-intact high-screen-quality lines as a specificity control. Do not repeat completed broad driver-gene screening unless new evidence justifies it. Future validation should be reserved for newly returned discoveries with discovery lower bounds near or above clinical thresholds, especially if they open a new axis or substantially refine the USP7 specificity question."
